# AIForge environment — copy to `.env` and edit:  cp .env.example .env
# `./run.sh` sources this automatically. Lines are KEY=value (no export/quotes).
# NEVER commit a real `.env` — it's gitignored.
#
# SINGLE MODE: everything runs on the host with embedded SQLite + scoped-OKR
# Markdown memory + Aider RepoMap + CodeGraph. No Postgres/Neo4j/Docker.
# The ONLY thing you MUST set is the model endpoint below.

# ──────────────────────────── Model endpoint (REQUIRED) ──────────────
# OpenAI-compatible base_url for your model. LM Studio / vLLM / Ollama /
# llama.cpp / TGI / LiteLLM all work. Use https:// for a TLS-fronted box.
AIFORGE_LM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
# API key for the endpoint (blank for keyless local servers).
# AIFORGE_LM_API_KEY=

# Per-role model endpoints (optional — one served model per role):
#   AIFORGE_DOER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
#   AIFORGE_PLANNER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1235/v1
#   AIFORGE_OPENAI_COMPAT_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1   # fallback for all roles
#   AIFORGE_OPENAI_COMPAT_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────── TLS / SSL ───────────────────────────
# Applies only to AIForge's own self-hosted services (model endpoint, MCP,
# local probes). Public hosts (GitHub, web fetches) are ALWAYS verified.
# Keep verify ON and trust an internal CA:
#   AIFORGE_LLM_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/internal-ca.pem
# OR relax it for an internal/LAN box you control (default: true):
AIFORGE_LLM_SSL_VERIFY=true

# ───────────────────────────── CodeGraph (optional) ──────────────────
# Comma-separated repo paths to index for the enforced codegraph_* tools.
# The binary auto-installs (npm @colbymchenry/codegraph). Empty = skip.
# AIFORGE_CODEGRAPH_REPOS=/path/repoA,/path/repoB
# AIFORGE_CODEGRAPH_BIN=/custom/path/to/codegraph   # if not on PATH

# ──────────────────────────── Tracing (optional) ─────────────────────
# Self-hosted Langfuse trace UI — the ONE optional Docker piece. Enable with
# `./run.sh --with-langfuse` or:
# AIFORGE_LANGFUSE=1

# ─────────────────────────────── Misc (optional) ─────────────────────
# Clamp the chat agent's filesystem scope (default: UNRESTRICTED host access):
# AIFORGE_WORKSPACE_DIR=/path/to/allowed/workspace
# Listen host/port (defaults 127.0.0.1:8799):
# AIFORGE_HOST=127.0.0.1
# AIFORGE_PORT=8799

# ─────────────────────── External Postgres (rare, opt-in) ────────────
# The app is SQLite-only by default. To use a real external Postgres INSTEAD,
# set BOTH (a stale AIFORGE_PG_URL alone is ignored):
# AIFORGE_KEEP_PG=1
# AIFORGE_PG_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/aiforge

# ── Shared memory: hub and spoke ──────────────────────────────────────
# Every machine compacts its own memory locally. ONE machine additionally runs
# the cross-machine merge: start that box with `./run.sh --admin` (which writes
# AIFORGE_ROLE=admin into this file, so a restart cannot silently demote it —
# a demoted admin retires its merged fold). `./run.sh --spoke` hands the role
# back, which is how you MOVE the admin to another box.
#
# On every OTHER machine, name the admin here and leave AIFORGE_ROLE unset:
# AIFORGE_ADMIN_URL=http://rig.lan:8799
#
# The admin's peer id is learned from its first reply and cached in
# ~/.aiforge/admin.json. Pin it only if you would rather not trust that reply:
# AIFORGE_ADMIN_ID=rig
#
# The sync surface (/api/memory/sync/*) answers WITHOUT a credential by
# default, so a spoke needs no secret. That is only safe on a trusted
# interface — a LAN or a WireGuard address. To require the API token on it
# too, set this on the admin AND set the same AIFORGE_API_TOKEN on every spoke:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_AUTH=1

# ── Groups: one admin, several independent fleets ─────────────────────
# Leave all of this unset and the admin serves ONE pool of knowledge, exactly
# as it always has. Set it on the ADMIN to serve several — different customers,
# different sites — each with its own tree that no other group can read:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_GROUPS=cellular,retail
#
# Groups can also be created at runtime (POST /api/admin/groups). Once the file
# exists it is the record, and this line is ignored — so an operator who has
# added a group through the API is not overwritten by a stale line here.
#
# A CLIENT discovers the list from the admin url it already has. One group is
# joined automatically; several means it asks, in Settings -> Memory sync, and
# sends NOTHING until one is picked (knowledge in the wrong pool cannot be
# taken back). Pin it instead on a headless box that will never see that screen:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_GROUP=cellular
#
# Before every fold and every pull that changes something, the received tree is
# snapshotted by hardlink so it can be rolled back
# (GET/POST /api/admin/memory/snapshots|revert). How many to keep:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_SNAPSHOTS=10

# ── What a machine will NOT send ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every client filters its own notes before advertising them, so credentials
# and idle searches ("what is the capital of France") never leave the machine
# that learned them. A note carrying a credential is held back WHOLE rather
# than edited, because the sentence around a secret usually identifies the
# system too. Settings -> Memory sync shows what was held back and why.
#
# The two tunable thresholds, both applied only to notes carrying no sign of
# the codebase — no file path, no identifier, no error string. Raise them if
# chatter is getting through; lower them if real notes are being held back:
# AIFORGE_FILTER_MIN_SUBSTANCE=80
# AIFORGE_FILTER_MIN_WORDS=8

# ── Predicting the next step ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At the end of a chat turn one capped call works out the action you most
# likely want next, and either DOES it or OFFERS it as a chip under the reply.
# Acting needs two things, not one: the model must be confident AND the action
# must be safe and reversible. Reads and safe shell commands act; a file write
# or a commit acts only on a clean git tree, so "undo" still means something;
# anything that leaves the machine, spends money or cannot be undone is ALWAYS
# only offered, at any confidence.
#
# Turn it off entirely — the turn then ends exactly as it did before, and no
# call is made at all:
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_DISABLE=1
#
# Keep the suggestions but never let them act on their own ("always ask me"):
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_ACT=0
#
# Below this confidence nothing is shown at all, not even an offer: a guess the
# model itself doubts is noise, and noise teaches you to ignore the chip that
# matters.
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.75
#
# The cap on that one call, and which model role makes it. It runs on a cheap
# role so the agent's own model is not tied up producing it.
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_TIMEOUT_S=10
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_ROLE=enhancer
#
# What you ACCEPT is remembered per repo and shown to the next prediction —
# that is the whole learning mechanism. Dismissals are recorded too, because a
# feature that learns only from its wins drifts. Credentials never reach that
# store: every row passes the same filter the memory sync uses, and a row it
# refuses is dropped rather than stored scrubbed.

# ── Outbound identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Model calls identify themselves as `aiforge/<version> (<your-login-name>)`, so
# a gateway operator can tell whose traffic is whose and which build sent it.
# Set this to override the whole string (a proxy/WAF that demands a specific
# audit value), or to `off` to send only `aiforge/<version>` with no username.
# AIFORGE_LLM_USER_AGENT=off
